Wake a connection right after a send batch, so loss recovery timers stay armed.

Applies to picoquic 70b5fd5aafdd2d6c349ff783202ece9d778bcaf2, after 0001 to
0010.

picoquic_prepare_packet_ex fills a send batch (GSO) packet by packet, and from
the second packet on takes the fast path, which computes no loss recovery or
ACK timers. When the last attempt sends nothing because the congestion window
is full, the connection keeps the fast path's wake time, often the keep-alive
or idle deadline. If the whole flight is then lost, no ACK wakes it and the
sender stays silent until that deadline (4 to 15 seconds observed under 5%
loss), where RFC 9002 Section 6.2 expects a probe within one PTO. A batch that
sent a packet now wakes the connection at once, so the next pass runs the full
checks.
Tests (picoquic_ct): batch_flight_loss, which fails without the sender.c
change.

